(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','LAUNDRY ROOM — Machine 6 is out of order. Card refunds for interrupted cycles are issued at the front desk of Hall A only, and only within one week of the incident; coins cannot be refunded.','NONE','["A card refund requires a trip to the Hall A desk within one week","Refunds continue forever at any service desk","Coins are refunded on the spot","Machine 6 is permanently closed"]','A',1,'Refunds for interrupted cycles are available only at the Hall A desk and only within one week, so claiming one means going there in time.',16),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','Nina: "Rehearsal moved to the black box theatre — the main stage is being repainted." — Sam: "Same time?" — Nina: "Six p.m., but the side entrance will be the only one open."','NONE','["Rehearse on the main stage","Enter through the side door of the black box theatre at the usual 6 p.m.","Help repaint the black box theatre","Arrive earlier than the usual 6 p.m."]','B',1,'The venue changes to the black box theatre reached by its side door while the time stays at 6 p.m. — the option about entering through the side door at the usual time.',17),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','CAMPUS ALERT — Because of an ice storm, all campus buildings will open two hours late on Wednesday. Dining halls keep their normal hours, and online classes continue as scheduled.','NONE','["Dining halls open two hours late","In-person and online classes both start late","Campus closes for the entire day","Different facilities begin Wednesday on different schedules rather than one uniform delay"]','D',1,'Buildings open late, dining stays normal and online classes run on time — three different schedules rather than a single delay.',18),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign and answer the question.','BIKE RENTAL — Residents rent free with a valid campus ID; visitors pay $3 per hour with a maximum of $15 per day. Helmets are included with every rental at no extra cost.','NONE','["A resident riding for two hours","Anyone, resident or visitor","A visitor buying a helmet","A visitor riding for two hours"]','D',1,'Visitors pay $3 per hour, so two hours costs $6; residents ride free and helmets are never an extra charge.',19),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice to tenants and answer the question.','NOTE TO TENANTS — The water supply to all floors above the ground floor will be shut off Thursday from 9:00 a.m. to 1:00 p.m. for valve work. Ground-floor apartments are exempt because their line was replaced last spring; store drinking water in advance.','NONE','["Upper-floor residents lose water for part of Thursday","All residents lose water on Thursday","The valve work will last two days","Ground-floor residents must store the most water"]','A',1,'Only floors above the exempt ground floor are cut off, and only Thursday morning into early afternoon.',20),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','STUDENT HEALTH EMAIL — Your vaccination record shows one of the two required doses missing. Upload a photo of your card through the portal, or attend the weekly walk-in clinic on Thursdays from 1:00 to 4:00 p.m.; no appointment is needed.','NONE','["Ignore the email if the card is at home","Submit the record only by postal mail","Go to the Thursday walk-in clinic without booking","Pay a processing fee at the portal"]','C',1,'Either a portal upload or the Thursday walk-in clinic resolves the gap, and the clinic explicitly requires no appointment.',21),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the whiteboard note and answer the question.','STUDIO KEY RULES — Sign the key out by 8:00 a.m. or your name moves down the rotation. Returning it later than 11:00 p.m. costs a $10 penalty and suspends next week''s eligibility.','NONE','["Nothing — the penalty applies only to late pick-up","A $10 fee and loss of priority for the following week","Permanent removal from the rotation","An automatic reminder sent at 11:00 p.m."]','B',1,'Returning after 11:00 p.m. brings the $10 penalty and suspends the next week''s eligibility — a fee and loss of priority for the following week.',22),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the board and answer the question.','CAFETERIA BOARD — Soup and the salad bar are served all day every weekday. The grilled option runs from 11:00 a.m. to 2:00 p.m. only, and the pasta special appears at Thursday lunch exclusively.','NONE','["The pasta special","Grilled food and salad only","Grilled food alone","Soup and salad, but nothing grilled"]','D',1,'At 3:00 p.m. on a Friday the grilled option has ended and pasta exists only on Thursdays, leaving just the all-day items.',23),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the group chat and answer the question.','Mei: "Professor Aris confirmed the midterm covers weeks 1 through 6, and week 7 only from the reading — no lecture detail." — Jon: "So we do not need to rewatch the week-7 video?" — Mei: "Correct, but skim the chapter."','NONE','["Week 7 appears only through its assigned reading","Only week 7 will be tested","The week-7 lecture video is required in full","Weeks 1 to 6 are optional"]','A',1,'Weeks 1-6 count fully while week 7 counts only from the reading, so the lecture video is not needed.',24),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the parking office update and answer the question.','PARKING PERMIT UPDATE — Annual permits now renew automatically unless you cancel before February 15. A semester permit converts to an annual one at a discount only during the first two weeks of term.','NONE','["Permits must be renewed every week","An annual permit renews by itself unless it is cancelled in time","The February deadline was moved earlier","Semester permits can never be discounted"]','B',1,'Automatic renewal means a driver who forgets to cancel before February 15 is charged again without doing anything.',25),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the museum notice and answer the question.','MUSEUM NOTICE — Timed tickets for the special exhibition are released online at 9:00 a.m. each Monday, one month in advance, and sell out within minutes; same-day exhibition tickets are not sold. The general collection stays open daily until 6:00 p.m. without a ticket.','NONE','["Entry to the special exhibition if they arrive early","A same-day timed ticket at the desk","A visit to the general collection on any day before 6:00 p.m.","A refund of the online booking fee"]','C',1,'Only the general collection is ticket-free and open daily until 6:00, so that visit is what a person without a timed ticket can still do.',26),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the form instruction and answer the question.','TRANSFER CREDIT FORM — Box 4 must contain the credit total from your most recent transcript, not the number of courses. A form that counts courses will be returned unprocessed.','NONE','["Nothing — twelve is twelve either way","The form is approved automatically","The registrar converts courses into credits for the student","The form is sent back without being evaluated"]','D',1,'Box 4 requires credits, so writing a course count makes the office return the form unprocessed.',27),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','Omar: "Studio bookings open Friday at noon and close again after 48 hours." — Lena: "I got the heads-up email this morning, so I will apply when I read it properly." — Omar: "Careful — the email is only notice; the system does not accept anything before noon Friday."','NONE','["The length of the booking window","The moment the system actually accepts bookings","The location of the studios","The fee for reserving a studio"]','B',1,'Omar is correcting when reservations open — Friday at noon, not when the morning email arrives.',28),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the gym notice and answer the question.','INDUCTION CARDS — Members whose card has lapsed must re-book the full 40-minute safety induction; the 10-minute refresher is offered only while a card is still valid. Until re-certified, access is limited to the pool deck.','NONE','["Take a short refresher instead","Keep access to every area","Retake the full induction, with only pool-deck access meanwhile","Have the old card reactivated at the front desk"]','C',1,'A lapsed member loses the refresher option, must repeat the 40-minute induction, and keeps only pool-deck access until finishing it.',29),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the bus card and answer the question.','ROUTE 9 — Buses run every 15 minutes on weekdays until 7:00 p.m., then every 30 minutes for the rest of the evening. On Sunday the first departure is 9:00 a.m.','NONE','["Fifteen minutes","One hour","Up to about half an hour","No bus until morning"]','C',1,'After 7:00 p.m. on a weekday the frequency drops to 30 minutes, so the longest wait is roughly half an hour.',30),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the printing policy and answer the question.','PRINTING QUOTA — Every student receives $30 of print credit per term. Unused fall credit rolls into spring only if at least one job runs during December; balances of students who never printed in December are forfeited.','NONE','["By spending the whole balance before Thanksgiving","By paying a roll-over fee","By printing in both autumn and summer","By running at least one print job during December"]','D',1,'December activity is the stated condition for keeping the balance, so one December job protects the credit.',31),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the club fair flyer and answer the question.','CLUB FAIR — Table requests arrive in order of receipt and are free; the two covered pavilions carry a $20 fee and are booked separately. Any table without a sponsor signature is dismantled on setup day.','NONE','["It must be one of the covered pavilions","It needs a sponsor signature or it will be taken down on setup day","It costs $20 whenever the weather turns","It must be requested after the fair opens"]','B',1,'The sponsor signature is the requirement stated for every table; pavilions are the paid option rather than the free one.',32),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the archive email and answer the question.','FROM THE ARCHIVE OFFICE — Your reading-room request for box 41 is confirmed for Tuesday at 10:00 a.m. Boxes must be ordered at least two working days ahead, and pencils only are permitted at the tables; ink of any kind is forbidden.','NONE','["A pencil and a blank sheet of paper","A fountain pen for notes","Any device that contains ink","The box itself, taken home that evening"]','A',1,'Pencils are the only permitted writing instrument and materials never leave the room, so a pencil with paper is the allowed combination.',33),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the texts and answer the question.','Devon: "The shuttle pass expires the day tuition is due, not the last day of classes." — Rosa: "Mine says valid until May 30, and my last planned ride is the 28th." — Devon: "Then you are covered for the move."','NONE','["Her tuition deadline passed in April","Passes stop working on the last day of class","The pass lasts beyond her final planned rides","She can extend it at any kiosk"]','C',1,'Her pass runs to May 30 while her last ride is May 28, so the validity covers all the travel she planned.',34),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the hall notice and answer the question.','RESIDENCE HALL — Quiet hours begin at 10:00 p.m. Sunday through Thursday. During exam weeks the start may shift to 11:00 p.m., but only if the hall senate votes for it; this term the motion failed by two voices.','NONE','["start one hour later","are suspended completely","move to midnight","begin at 10:00 p.m. as usual"]','D',1,'Because the vote to extend failed, the standard 10:00 p.m. quiet hours stay in force even during exam weeks.',35),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?','The price of a gemstone looks like a crude violation of supply and demand: diamonds sell for far more than their physical scarcity alone would predict, and the explanation lies in how the market was constructed. For most of the twentieth century a single syndicate released stones from hidden stockpiles in measured quantities, holding supply below demand and prices above competitive levels. A second mechanism proved just as powerful. Because buyers cannot judge a gem unaided, they read price itself as a signal of quality, and decades of advertising fixed the stone in the public mind as the essential token of engagement. Economists call goods whose demand rises with price Veblen goods, and diamonds remain the purest example. When rival producers finally flooded the market with cheaper stones from new mines, the syndicate did not cut prices; it re-branded its own output as responsibly sourced, demonstrating that the most valuable asset it controlled was a story rather than geology.','NONE','["New mines permanently eliminated the possibility of high gem prices","Ordinary buyers can usually judge a gem''s quality unaided","Diamond prices are explained largely by deliberate control of supply and by the meanings attached to the stones","The syndicate''s advertising produced more revenue than its stockpiles ever did"]','C',1,'The passage centers on two mechanisms that sustained high prices — restricted supply and the price-as-quality signal — which is the option about deliberate supply control and attached meanings.',36),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "fixed" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','The price of a gemstone looks like a crude violation of supply and demand: diamonds sell for far more than their physical scarcity alone would predict, and the explanation lies in how the market was constructed. For most of the twentieth century a single syndicate released stones from hidden stockpiles in measured quantities, holding supply below demand and prices above competitive levels. A second mechanism proved just as powerful. Because buyers cannot judge a gem unaided, they read price itself as a signal of quality, and decades of advertising fixed the stone in the public mind as the essential token of engagement. Economists call goods whose demand rises with price Veblen goods, and diamonds remain the purest example. When rival producers finally flooded the market with cheaper stones from new mines, the syndicate did not cut prices; it re-branded its own output as responsibly sourced, demonstrating that the most valuable asset it controlled was a story rather than geology.','NONE','["attached physically","established firmly","repaired permanently","fastened by a legal contract"]','B',1,'Advertising set the stone firmly in the public mind, so in this context "fixed" means established firmly, which is option B.',37),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about the syndicate''s decision to re-brand rather than cut prices?','The price of a gemstone looks like a crude violation of supply and demand: diamonds sell for far more than their physical scarcity alone would predict, and the explanation lies in how the market was constructed. For most of the twentieth century a single syndicate released stones from hidden stockpiles in measured quantities, holding supply below demand and prices above competitive levels. A second mechanism proved just as powerful. Because buyers cannot judge a gem unaided, they read price itself as a signal of quality, and decades of advertising fixed the stone in the public mind as the essential token of engagement. Economists call goods whose demand rises with price Veblen goods, and diamonds remain the purest example. When rival producers finally flooded the market with cheaper stones from new mines, the syndicate did not cut prices; it re-branded its own output as responsibly sourced, demonstrating that the most valuable asset it controlled was a story rather than geology.','NONE','["It conceded that price competition was the only remaining weapon","It relied on a source of value that did not depend on the stones'' physical scarcity","It proved that buyers ignore ethical claims entirely","It showed that Veblen goods can never stay expensive"]','B',1,'Choosing a new story over a price cut is presented as proof that the syndicate''s key asset was the narrative rather than geology — the option about value not depending on physical scarcity.',38),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, how does methylation change gene activity without altering the genome?','Every cell of a human body carries the same genome, yet a liver cell and a neuron behave differently because they read different subsets of it. One mechanism governing this selection is methylation: the attachment of a small chemical tag, a methyl group, to cytosine bases at particular sites along the DNA strand. The tag does not rewrite the genetic code any more than a bookmark rewrites a novel; instead it changes which passages are accessible, typically quieting the gene near which it accumulates. Methylation patterns are laid down during embryonic development and largely maintained each time a cell divides, which is why a skin cell produces a skin cell. The pattern is not absolute: nutrition and chronic stress can alter it, and a few marks appear to cross the germ line, allowing experience to influence the next generation without touching the sequence. Researchers therefore call methylation epigenetic regulation — control that operates on top of, rather than inside, the inherited code.','NONE','["It tags the strand so that certain genes become less accessible to the reading machinery","It deletes the genes that a cell does not need","It permanently rewrites the order of the DNA bases","It converts one cell type into another during development"]','A',1,'The passage compares the methyl tag to a bookmark that changes which passages are accessible, quieting nearby genes — the option about tagging the strand and reducing accessibility.',39),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "quieting" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Every cell of a human body carries the same genome, yet a liver cell and a neuron behave differently because they read different subsets of it. One mechanism governing this selection is methylation: the attachment of a small chemical tag, a methyl group, to cytosine bases at particular sites along the DNA strand. The tag does not rewrite the genetic code any more than a bookmark rewrites a novel; instead it changes which passages are accessible, typically quieting the gene near which it accumulates. Methylation patterns are laid down during embryonic development and largely maintained each time a cell divides, which is why a skin cell produces a skin cell. The pattern is not absolute: nutrition and chronic stress can alter it, and a few marks appear to cross the germ line, allowing experience to influence the next generation without touching the sequence. Researchers therefore call methylation epigenetic regulation — control that operates on top of, rather than inside, the inherited code.','NONE','["activating","copying","suppressing","repairing"]','C',1,'The tag reduces the activity of the gene beside it, so "quieting" means suppressing — option C.',40),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage suggest about methylation patterns?','Every cell of a human body carries the same genome, yet a liver cell and a neuron behave differently because they read different subsets of it. One mechanism governing this selection is methylation: the attachment of a small chemical tag, a methyl group, to cytosine bases at particular sites along the DNA strand. The tag does not rewrite the genetic code any more than a bookmark rewrites a novel; instead it changes which passages are accessible, typically quieting the gene near which it accumulates. Methylation patterns are laid down during embryonic development and largely maintained each time a cell divides, which is why a skin cell produces a skin cell. The pattern is not absolute: nutrition and chronic stress can alter it, and a few marks appear to cross the germ line, allowing experience to influence the next generation without touching the sequence. Researchers therefore call methylation epigenetic regulation — control that operates on top of, rather than inside, the inherited code.','NONE','["They are laid down only after birth","They can never be changed once written","They are reset every time a cell divides","They usually persist through cell division yet remain partly responsive to life experience"]','D',1,'The passage says patterns are largely maintained through division but can be altered by nutrition and stress, matching the option about persistence with partial responsiveness.',41),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','Why, according to the passage, did the events of 1858 lead so quickly to a building programme?','The summer of 1858 is remembered as the year smell overtook legislation. The Thames, carrying the untreated waste of two million Londoners, lay exposed along the banks of Parliament at low tide, and a heat wave turned the river into what a newspaper called a pestilential ditch. Members soaked their curtain rods with chloride; committees sat with windows open onto the water rather than endure the lobbies. The result was an unusually swift decision. Joseph Bazalgette, an engineer who had spent years arguing for an intercepting system, received funding and authority almost at once. His design captured sewage in great brick conduits along the embankments and carried it by gravity to outfalls far downstream, where the tide diluted the discharge. The engineering was conservative — brick, gravity, wide bore — but its geography was radical, moving waste away from the city''s heart instead of merely hiding it. Commentators credited the sewers with ending cholera; the reasoning was wrong, since the disease travelled in water no nose could detect, yet the death rates did fall.','NONE','["The smell struck the legislators themselves, converting a long-delayed plan into urgent law","A cholera outbreak had already reached the buildings of Parliament","The engineer personally financed the first season of construction","New chemical techniques made treatment far cheaper than expected"]','A',1,'The passage explains that the stench physically reached Parliament, which is why approval was swift — the option about the smell striking the legislators.',42),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "outfalls" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','The summer of 1858 is remembered as the year smell overtook legislation. The Thames, carrying the untreated waste of two million Londoners, lay exposed along the banks of Parliament at low tide, and a heat wave turned the river into what a newspaper called a pestilential ditch. Members soaked their curtain rods with chloride; committees sat with windows open onto the water rather than endure the lobbies. The result was an unusually swift decision. Joseph Bazalgette, an engineer who had spent years arguing for an intercepting system, received funding and authority almost at once. His design captured sewage in great brick conduits along the embankments and carried it by gravity to outfalls far downstream, where the tide diluted the discharge. The engineering was conservative — brick, gravity, wide bore — but its geography was radical, moving waste away from the city''s heart instead of merely hiding it. Commentators credited the sewers with ending cholera; the reasoning was wrong, since the disease travelled in water no nose could detect, yet the death rates did fall.','NONE','["places where something is released","sections that can be locked","routes along the embankment","brick-lined storage vaults"]','A',1,'The conduits carried sewage to points downstream where it was discharged into the tide, so "outfalls" means places where something is released.',43),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage imply about the nineteenth-century claim that the sewers ended cholera?','The summer of 1858 is remembered as the year smell overtook legislation. The Thames, carrying the untreated waste of two million Londoners, lay exposed along the banks of Parliament at low tide, and a heat wave turned the river into what a newspaper called a pestilential ditch. Members soaked their curtain rods with chloride; committees sat with windows open onto the water rather than endure the lobbies. The result was an unusually swift decision. Joseph Bazalgette, an engineer who had spent years arguing for an intercepting system, received funding and authority almost at once. His design captured sewage in great brick conduits along the embankments and carried it by gravity to outfalls far downstream, where the tide diluted the discharge. The engineering was conservative — brick, gravity, wide bore — but its geography was radical, moving waste away from the city''s heart instead of merely hiding it. Commentators credited the sewers with ending cholera; the reasoning was wrong, since the disease travelled in water no nose could detect, yet the death rates did fall.','NONE','["It exaggerated a change that never really happened","It rested on correct facts about how the disease travelled","It was mistaken about the cause yet the sewers genuinely removed the contaminated water behind the disease","It was proposed by Bazalgette himself to justify the cost"]','C',1,'The passage calls the reasoning wrong (the disease travelled in water, not smell) while noting deaths did fall because the water was removed — the option about a mistaken cause but a real benefit.',44),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','Which of the following is the main idea of the passage?','During an ice age a continental ice sheet several kilometres thick piles onto the crust as though an ocean were being set on a table. The crust responds like a slow raft floating on the hot, creeping rock of the mantle beneath, sagging into it while the load persists. Remove the ice and the logic runs in reverse: freed of its burden, the land rises again, a process called isostatic rebound. The recovery is measured not in years but in millennia, because mantle rock, though technically solid, flows under sustained pressure. The evidence is written into coastlines. Around the Gulf of Bothnia, raised shorelines stand metres above the present water line and can be ordered by their height into a sequence, letting geologists chart an uplift that continues today at several millimetres a year. This uplift also bends the map of relative sea level: a rising shore makes the sea appear to fall locally even while the global ocean climbs, so northern coasts dry out while distant shores, which receive the redistributed water, flood sooner.','NONE','["Ice sheets form several kilometres thick because the mantle is hot","Relative sea level is impossible to measure near the Baltic","The crust slowly sinks under an ice sheet and rebounds after it melts, with visible effects on coastlines worldwide","Erosion rather than ice explains the shorelines of the Gulf of Bothnia"]','C',1,'The passage traces loading, rebound and their coastal consequences, which is the option stating that the crust sinks under ice and rebounds with worldwide shoreline effects.',45),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "raised" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','During an ice age a continental ice sheet several kilometres thick piles onto the crust as though an ocean were being set on a table. The crust responds like a slow raft floating on the hot, creeping rock of the mantle beneath, sagging into it while the load persists. Remove the ice and the logic runs in reverse: freed of its burden, the land rises again, a process called isostatic rebound. The recovery is measured not in years but in millennia, because mantle rock, though technically solid, flows under sustained pressure. The evidence is written into coastlines. Around the Gulf of Bothnia, raised shorelines stand metres above the present water line and can be ordered by their height into a sequence, letting geologists chart an uplift that continues today at several millimetres a year. This uplift also bends the map of relative sea level: a rising shore makes the sea appear to fall locally even while the global ocean climbs, so northern coasts dry out while distant shores, which receive the redistributed water, flood sooner.','NONE','["elevated","submerged","eroded","painted"]','A',1,'The old shorelines now stand metres above the water because the land lifted, so "raised" means elevated.',46),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about relative sea level from the passage?','During an ice age a continental ice sheet several kilometres thick piles onto the crust as though an ocean were being set on a table. The crust responds like a slow raft floating on the hot, creeping rock of the mantle beneath, sagging into it while the load persists. Remove the ice and the logic runs in reverse: freed of its burden, the land rises again, a process called isostatic rebound. The recovery is measured not in years but in millennia, because mantle rock, though technically solid, flows under sustained pressure. The evidence is written into coastlines. Around the Gulf of Bothnia, raised shorelines stand metres above the present water line and can be ordered by their height into a sequence, letting geologists chart an uplift that continues today at several millimetres a year. This uplift also bends the map of relative sea level: a rising shore makes the sea appear to fall locally even while the global ocean climbs, so northern coasts dry out while distant shores, which receive the redistributed water, flood sooner.','NONE','["It always matches the growth of the global ocean","It rises at every coast once ice sheets melt","It is measured only from satellites","It can drop at one coastline while the world''s ocean volume is increasing"]','D',1,'The passage states a rising shore makes the sea appear to fall locally even while the global ocean climbs, so relative sea level can drop somewhere as oceans rise overall.',47),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The passage is primarily concerned with','When a manuscript leaves an editor for its referees, most readers assume the reviewer''s name is concealed; the older and stranger practice was to conceal the author''s instead. Before the middle of the twentieth century, referees were chosen partly by institutional rank: a junior critic attacking a senior figure''s argument risked career damage, while an editor reading a famous name judged it more generously. Hiding the author''s identity shifts attention to the claim itself — the same method described in the same paragraph should earn the same verdict whether the paper arrives from a small southern laboratory or from the department that trained the referee. Experiments have tested the theory with mixed success: reviewers often guess identities from citation habits and shared datasets, and blinding can disadvantage work whose methods are necessarily local. Advocates of open review reply that a signed referee has more at stake and criticises more carefully. What almost nobody defends is the alternative — an editor judging everything alone. Blindness is imperfect justice, chosen over no scrutiny at all.','NONE','["explaining how editors select referees for a journal","defending a return to named, signed review","setting out why anonymous review exists and conceding its limits","proving that institutional rank no longer shapes judgement"]','C',1,'The passage gives the reasons for blinding and then details its failures and the counterproposal, matching the option about explaining the practice while acknowledging its limits.',48),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "verdict" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','When a manuscript leaves an editor for its referees, most readers assume the reviewer''s name is concealed; the older and stranger practice was to conceal the author''s instead. Before the middle of the twentieth century, referees were chosen partly by institutional rank: a junior critic attacking a senior figure''s argument risked career damage, while an editor reading a famous name judged it more generously. Hiding the author''s identity shifts attention to the claim itself — the same method described in the same paragraph should earn the same verdict whether the paper arrives from a small southern laboratory or from the department that trained the referee. Experiments have tested the theory with mixed success: reviewers often guess identities from citation habits and shared datasets, and blinding can disadvantage work whose methods are necessarily local. Advocates of open review reply that a signed referee has more at stake and criticises more carefully. What almost nobody defends is the alternative — an editor judging everything alone. Blindness is imperfect justice, chosen over no scrutiny at all.','NONE','["summary","signature","invitation","assessment"]','D',1,'The point is that identical work should receive the same evaluation regardless of its source, so "verdict" means assessment.',49),
(@set08_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage suggest about open (signed) review?','When a manuscript leaves an editor for its referees, most readers assume the reviewer''s name is concealed; the older and stranger practice was to conceal the author''s instead. Before the middle of the twentieth century, referees were chosen partly by institutional rank: a junior critic attacking a senior figure''s argument risked career damage, while an editor reading a famous name judged it more generously. Hiding the author''s identity shifts attention to the claim itself — the same method described in the same paragraph should earn the same verdict whether the paper arrives from a small southern laboratory or from the department that trained the referee. Experiments have tested the theory with mixed success: reviewers often guess identities from citation habits and shared datasets, and blinding can disadvantage work whose methods are necessarily local. Advocates of open review reply that a signed referee has more at stake and criticises more carefully. What almost nobody defends is the alternative — an editor judging everything alone. Blindness is imperfect justice, chosen over no scrutiny at all.','NONE','["Its supporters expect a referee''s signature to sharpen rather than soften criticism","It removes every possibility of guessing who wrote a paper","Most journals have now adopted it in place of blinding","It is the arrangement that existed before the mid-twentieth century"]','A',1,'The passage says proponents argue a signed referee "has more at stake and criticises more carefully", i.e. that openness sharpens criticism.',50);
INSERT INTO tp_questions (exam_id, section, task_type, prompt, body_text, audio_mode, choices_json, answer_key, auto_score, explanation, order_no) VALUES
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Your defence needs three readers — who has already agreed?','TTS','["Two, and I am asking the third today.","At three o''clock, if that works.","Yes, three is my number.","It agreed to meet in June."]','A',1,'The speaker asks who has agreed; option A answers directly by reporting that two readers have agreed and a third is being asked.',51),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Your defence clashes with my conference talk — could we push it to the following week?','TTS','["The defence needs three readers.","Yes, the room is already booked.","I would rather not — I have a departmental commitment that week; how about the one after?","That was a long talk."]','C',1,'A request to reschedule is best met with agreement or a polite counter-proposal; option C declines that week and suggests the next.',52),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Should I book the defence room through the portal or ask the events office?','TTS','["Yes, at two in the afternoon.","Ask the events office — the portal is down this month.","I booked it, but they said to cancel.","No, the room was not free."]','B',1,'An either/or question is answered by picking one route; option B recommends the events office and explains why.',53),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Conference registration closes the day before — have you got your receipt yet?','TTS','["Just now, it arrived by email.","At the hotel, on the third floor.","Next Wednesday, I believe.","It was free for students."]','A',1,'A yes/no question about receiving the receipt is answered by option A, which confirms it arrived by email.',54),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Is your badge for the main track or the workshop day?','TTS','["I am wearing the blue one.","You have to register first.","The hall opens at nine.","Main track — the workshop sessions are already full."]','D',1,'An either/or question about the badge needs one of the two options; option D picks the main track and adds a reason.',55),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Do you know which session the keynote is in?','TTS','["A keynote is always in the morning.","Room 104, but the poster session moved.","It is the plenary at four on Tuesday, I think.","Did you register for it?"]','C',1,'The question asks for a specific session; option C names the plenary at four on Tuesday.',56),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','How soon after submission will we hear the funding decision?','TTS','["To the grants portal, I think.","Roughly six weeks, they said.","Yes, the budget is small.","I submitted it last spring."]','B',1,'A how-soon question asks for a length of time; option B supplies roughly six weeks.',57),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','The portal says the budget table must be attached — have you uploaded it?','TTS','["It is somewhere in section two.","Yes, it went up last night.","Mandatory — applications without it come back unread.","No, the cost is small."]','B',1,'The yes/no question about uploading is answered directly by option B, which confirms the file went up last night.',58),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Your proposal lists equipment costs in year two — is that when the purchase happens?','TTS','["Costs go in a separate table.","Two years, if the equipment lasts.","In the first half of year two, yes.","Section four, at the very end."]','C',1,'A yes/no question about timing is confirmed by option C, which places the purchase in the first half of year two.',59),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','The archive requires white gloves for the collection — shall I bring mine to the desk?','TTS','["The desk is on the left.","No, the reading room supplies them, but thank you.","The collection is open until five.","I will bring a pencil too."]','B',1,'An offer to bring gloves is met by option B, which declines the offer and notes the room supplies them.',60),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Do I need an appointment for the medieval manuscripts, or can I just walk in?','TTS','["Medieval scripts were mostly written in Latin.","Only on Monday mornings.","I will bring my own gloves.","An appointment is needed two days ahead — the boxes come from a closed store."]','D',1,'The either/or question about appointments is answered by option D, which states the two-day booking rule and its reason.',61),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the statement and choose the best response.','Remember, the reading room forbids ink — pencils only at the tables.','TTS','["They open at nine.","About two hundred pages, I think.","Yes, good thing I left my pen in the car.","No, I will return them on Friday."]','C',1,'A reminder about the no-ink rule is best acknowledged by option C, which notes the speaker''s pen stayed in the car.',62),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','When does your lab induction certificate actually expire?','TTS','["Inductions run twice a month.","Yes, I will renew it.","The lab is on the third floor.","In the last week of August."]','D',1,'A when-question asks for a time; option D gives the last week of August.',63),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the statement and choose the best response.','If you miss the re-certification session, you cannot sign in alone — is that clear?','TTS','["I signed in last month.","Understood — I will book the make-up session now.","It runs about three hours.","No, the door code still works."]','B',1,'A check for understanding is answered by option B, which confirms understanding and books the make-up session.',64),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Do the fire drills count toward the safety re-certification hours?','TTS','["Every semester, apparently.","Drills start at ten o''clock.","Count the hours on the portal.","Only if you signed the attendance sheet."]','D',1,'A yes/no question about counting hours is answered conditionally by option D, which gives the attendance-sheet requirement.',65),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Could you tell us why you chose this particular research topic?','TTS','["Three references, of course.","It began with a dataset my supervisor left behind, and the gap in it drew me in.","At the university library, mostly.","I am available any afternoon."]','B',1,'A why-question in an interview is answered by option B, which explains the origin of the topic choice.',66),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Your teaching statement mentions a lab course — how large a group would you plan for?','TTS','["Yes, I do teach labs.","About two hours each week.","I would cap it at sixteen so every student gets bench time.","My statement is on the portal."]','C',1,'A how-large question asks for group size; option C gives a cap of sixteen students.',67),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Before we finish, do you have any questions about the position?','TTS','["Yes — what is the start date for shortlisted candidates?","Yes, that is correct.","The position is in the history department.","I finished my questions yesterday."]','A',1,'An invitation to ask questions is taken up by option A, which asks about the start date.',68),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation, then answer the question: What does the administrator say about the portal?','Student: I want to book my thesis defence for the week of March nine, but the scheduling portal will not show any rooms for that week. — Administrator: The portal releases rooms only two weeks in advance, and for defence rooms the graduate school also requires an approved committee form before the booking unlocks. — Student: My form was signed last month. Could you reserve a room by hand for me?','TTS','["It releases rooms only two weeks ahead.","It requires the signed form to be uploaded.","It stays closed for the whole week.","It books lecture rooms only"]','A',1,'The administrator states that the portal releases rooms two weeks in advance — option A.',69),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation, then answer the question: What is already true of the committee form?','Student: I want to book my thesis defence for the week of March nine, but the scheduling portal will not show any rooms for that week. — Administrator: The portal releases rooms only two weeks in advance, and for defence rooms the graduate school also requires an approved committee form before the booking unlocks. — Student: My form was signed last month. Could you reserve a room by hand for me?','TTS','["It still needs two signatures","It has been released from the portal","It was signed last month","It must be rewritten before Friday"]','C',1,'The student says the form was signed last month, so option C is the completed step.',70),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation, then answer the question: What does the student ask the administrator to do at the end?','Student: I want to book my thesis defence for the week of March nine, but the scheduling portal will not show any rooms for that week. — Administrator: The portal releases rooms only two weeks in advance, and for defence rooms the graduate school also requires an approved committee form before the booking unlocks. — Student: My form was signed last month. Could you reserve a room by hand for me?','TTS','["Extend the portal release window","Approve the committee form","Email the graduate school","Reserve a room outside the normal process"]','D',1,'The final line asks the administrator to reserve a room by hand, i.e. option D, outside the normal portal process.',71),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation, then answer the question: What problem does the man point out?','Man: Your conference badge shows main track, but you paid at the workshop rate — the two prices are different. — Woman: That is odd; I registered online in one session and never edited the form. — Man: The form sometimes defaults to the cheaper option. I can correct it now, but the difference must be paid before the workshop entry is released.','TTS','["The woman arrived too late to register","The badge category does not match the rate the woman paid","The online system has crashed","The workshop has already started"]','B',1,'The man notes that the badge says main track while the payment was the workshop rate — option B.',72),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation, then answer the question: What does the woman say about her registration?','Man: Your conference badge shows main track, but you paid at the workshop rate — the two prices are different. — Woman: That is odd; I registered online in one session and never edited the form. — Man: The form sometimes defaults to the cheaper option. I can correct it now, but the difference must be paid before the workshop entry is released.','TTS','["She filled it in but never paid","She submitted it at the front desk","She completed it in one session without editing it","She registered under a colleague''s name"]','C',1,'The woman states she registered in one online session and never edited the form — option C.',73),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation, then answer the question: What will happen if the man corrects the record now?','Man: Your conference badge shows main track, but you paid at the workshop rate — the two prices are different. — Woman: That is odd; I registered online in one session and never edited the form. — Man: The form sometimes defaults to the cheaper option. I can correct it now, but the difference must be paid before the workshop entry is released.','TTS','["Her hotel booking will be extended","She will receive a refund of the difference","The correction will be refused after noon","Workshop entry will stay locked until she pays the difference"]','D',1,'The man says the price difference must be paid before the workshop entry is released — option D.',74),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation, then answer the question: What does the clerk confirm about the maps?','Visitor: I emailed yesterday about the 1870s sewer maps, and someone said they are stored off-site. — Clerk: That is right. Off-site boxes take forty-eight hours to arrive, so a request sent yesterday afternoon could already be ready — the reading room closes at five. Please sign the request ledger and use a pencil at desk three.','TTS','["They are kept away from the main building","They cannot be shown to visitors","They were damaged long ago","They are available only online"]','A',1,'The clerk confirms the off-site storage, i.e. that the maps are kept away from the main building — option A.',75),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation, then answer the question: Why does the clerk mention the forty-eight-hour rule?','Visitor: I emailed yesterday about the 1870s sewer maps, and someone said they are stored off-site. — Clerk: That is right. Off-site boxes take forty-eight hours to arrive, so a request sent yesterday afternoon could already be ready — the reading room closes at five. Please sign the request ledger and use a pencil at desk three.','TTS','["To explain that the boxes may already have arrived","To complain about the courier service","To justify the five o''clock closing","To warn that the request came too late"]','A',1,'The clerk works the forty-eight-hour delivery against the visitor''s timing to show the boxes could already be ready — option A.',76),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation, then answer the question: What will the visitor most likely do next?','Visitor: I emailed yesterday about the 1870s sewer maps, and someone said they are stored off-site. — Clerk: That is right. Off-site boxes take forty-eight hours to arrive, so a request sent yesterday afternoon could already be ready — the reading room closes at five. Please sign the request ledger and use a pencil at desk three.','TTS','["Order a second box of maps","Send another confirmation email","Sign a ledger and work with a pencil at a desk","Leave and return on Friday"]','C',1,'The clerk''s closing instructions are to sign the ledger and use a pencil at desk three, so option C is the next step.',77),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation, then answer the question: Why must the student repeat the full safety session?','Technician: Because your induction card lapsed in June, you need the full safety session again, not the ten-minute refresher. — Student: But I only missed one lab this spring — is there really no shorter option? — Technician: The refresher is for current cards only. I can put you in next week''s session; until you finish it, your access stops at the pool deck.','TTS','["She lost her induction card","Her safety card expired in June","She missed last year''s fire drill","She has never attended an induction"]','B',1,'The technician opens by saying the card lapsed in June, which is why the full session is required — option B.',78),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation, then answer the question: What does the student imply about her own case?','Technician: Because your induction card lapsed in June, you need the full safety session again, not the ten-minute refresher. — Student: But I only missed one lab this spring — is there really no shorter option? — Technician: The refresher is for current cards only. I can put you in next week''s session; until you finish it, your access stops at the pool deck.','TTS','["She believes a lighter option should apply to her","She has already booked next week","She wants to drop the lab entirely","She doubts that the card ever lapsed"]','A',1,'By objecting that she missed only one lab and asking about a shorter option, the student implies option A — she expects a lighter path.',79),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation, then answer the question: What does the technician say about access before re-certification?','Technician: Because your induction card lapsed in June, you need the full safety session again, not the ten-minute refresher. — Student: But I only missed one lab this spring — is there really no shorter option? — Technician: The refresher is for current cards only. I can put you in next week''s session; until you finish it, your access stops at the pool deck.','TTS','["It is unchanged until the make-up session","It reaches only one area of the facilities","It depends on a new door code","It is granted by the committee"]','B',1,'The technician says access stops at the pool deck until the session is finished — one area only, option B.',80),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement, then answer the question: What is the announcement mainly about?','Attention, please. The special collections reading room will close at noon tomorrow while the ventilation system is serviced. Requests placed before eleven will be delivered during today; later requests will be filled on Friday. Hours on the regular floors are unaffected.','TTS','["A permanent move of the special collections","A one-day early closure of one library space","A new request system for rare boxes","The closing of every floor tomorrow"]','B',1,'The whole notice concerns tomorrow''s noon closure of the special collections room for servicing — option B.',81),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement, then answer the question: According to the announcement, what happens to requests placed after eleven tomorrow?','Attention, please. The special collections reading room will close at noon tomorrow while the ventilation system is serviced. Requests placed before eleven will be delivered during today; later requests will be filled on Friday. Hours on the regular floors are unaffected.','TTS','["They are cancelled automatically","They must be placed in person instead","They will be delivered today anyway","They will be filled on Friday"]','D',1,'The announcement states that later requests, those after eleven, will be filled on Friday — option D.',82),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement, then answer the question: What can be inferred about the ventilation service?','Attention, please. The special collections reading room will close at noon tomorrow while the ventilation system is serviced. Requests placed before eleven will be delivered during today; later requests will be filled on Friday. Hours on the regular floors are unaffected.','TTS','["It cannot be carried out while the room is open to readers","It will also affect the regular floors","It was postponed from last week","It will finish before eleven o''clock"]','A',1,'The room must close early for the work, so the service cannot run while readers are present — option A.',83),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement, then answer the question: What correction does the speaker give?','Good morning. A reminder that the laboratory safety re-certification begins at ten in the main lecture theatre, not in the lab as the older posters say. Attendance is compulsory for anyone whose card was issued before last autumn, and a second session runs next Thursday for those who cannot come today.','TTS','["The start time of the session","The name of the technician","The location of the session","The length of the session"]','C',1,'The speaker corrects the room: the session is in the main lecture theatre, not the lab as older posters said — option C.',84),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement, then answer the question: Who must attend the session?','Good morning. A reminder that the laboratory safety re-certification begins at ten in the main lecture theatre, not in the lab as the older posters say. Attendance is compulsory for anyone whose card was issued before last autumn, and a second session runs next Thursday for those who cannot come today.','TTS','["Everyone who works in any laboratory","Students whose cards were issued before last autumn","Students who missed last Thursday''s drill","All conference attendees"]','B',1,'Compulsory attendance is defined for card holders issued before last autumn — option B.',85),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement, then answer the question: What does the speaker say about the session next Thursday?','Good morning. A reminder that the laboratory safety re-certification begins at ten in the main lecture theatre, not in the lab as the older posters say. Attendance is compulsory for anyone whose card was issued before last autumn, and a second session runs next Thursday for those who cannot come today.','TTS','["It replaces today''s session entirely","It is limited to new students","It will be cancelled if few sign up","It is an alternative for those who cannot attend today"]','D',1,'The Thursday session is offered for those who cannot come today, making it an alternative — option D.',86),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement, then answer the question: Why is the registration desk moving?','Conference notice. The registration desk will move from the lobby to the east hall at lunchtime, because the lobby hosts a private event from two o''clock. Attendees collecting badges after one should use the east hall, while the workshop check-in table stays in the lobby as planned. No talk sessions are affected.','TTS','["The lobby is needed for another event","The registration system has failed","The east hall is quieter in the afternoon","Talk sessions have moved to the lobby"]','A',1,'The notice gives the reason directly: the lobby hosts a private event from two o''clock — option A.',87),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement, then answer the question: Which service stays in the original location?','Conference notice. The registration desk will move from the lobby to the east hall at lunchtime, because the lobby hosts a private event from two o''clock. Attendees collecting badges after one should use the east hall, while the workshop check-in table stays in the lobby as planned. No talk sessions are affected.','TTS','["Badge collection","The workshop check-in table","The keynote sessions","The lunch service"]','B',1,'The announcement says the workshop check-in table stays in the lobby — option B.',88),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement, then answer the question: Who does the speaker say is affected by the move?','Conference notice. The registration desk will move from the lobby to the east hall at lunchtime, because the lobby hosts a private event from two o''clock. Attendees collecting badges after one should use the east hall, while the workshop check-in table stays in the lobby as planned. No talk sessions are affected.','TTS','["Attendees arriving before lunch","Session chairs only","People collecting badges after 1:00 p.m.","Everyone who paid the workshop rate"]','C',1,'Badge collectors after one are directed to the east hall, so option C names the affected group.',89),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk, then answer the question: What is the talk mainly about?','Man: We usually treat price as a simple scoreboard of supply and demand, but there is a whole class of goods where the high price is itself part of the appeal. — Woman: You mean diamonds and handbags that cost more than their materials could ever explain? — Man: Exactly. Thorstein Veblen argued in 1899 that people sometimes buy things precisely because they are expensive, since the price broadcasts that the owner can afford to waste money. — Woman: So the demand curve bends backwards — raise the price and some buyers want it more. — Man: That is the odd part. Standard theory says a price rise thins the queue, but with these goods a price rise can leave the queue longer, because a cheaper version quietly stops being worth buying.','TTS','["How advertising budgets shape gem markets","Why governments tax luxury goods","A class of goods whose demand can rise along with their price","The history of the diamond syndicate"]','C',1,'The exchange centers on Veblen goods, items whose desire can grow with price — option C.',90),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk, then answer the question: What does standard theory predict about price increases?','Man: We usually treat price as a simple scoreboard of supply and demand, but there is a whole class of goods where the high price is itself part of the appeal. — Woman: You mean diamonds and handbags that cost more than their materials could ever explain? — Man: Exactly. Thorstein Veblen argued in 1899 that people sometimes buy things precisely because they are expensive, since the price broadcasts that the owner can afford to waste money. — Woman: So the demand curve bends backwards — raise the price and some buyers want it more. — Man: That is the odd part. Standard theory says a price rise thins the queue, but with these goods a price rise can leave the queue longer, because a cheaper version quietly stops being worth buying.','TTS','["They change advertising messages","They reduce the number of willing buyers","They raise production costs","They attract more competitors"]','B',1,'The man says standard theory expects a price rise to thin the queue, i.e. reduce willing buyers — option B.',91),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk, then answer the question: Why does the man say these goods can stay expensive despite cheaper rivals?','Man: We usually treat price as a simple scoreboard of supply and demand, but there is a whole class of goods where the high price is itself part of the appeal. — Woman: You mean diamonds and handbags that cost more than their materials could ever explain? — Man: Exactly. Thorstein Veblen argued in 1899 that people sometimes buy things precisely because they are expensive, since the price broadcasts that the owner can afford to waste money. — Woman: So the demand curve bends backwards — raise the price and some buyers want it more. — Man: That is the odd part. Standard theory says a price rise thins the queue, but with these goods a price rise can leave the queue longer, because a cheaper version quietly stops being worth buying.','TTS','["Competitors cannot copy the designs","Legal rules protect the original makers","Demand rests on a signal that a discount would destroy","Buyers cannot compare prices easily"]','C',1,'The closing line explains that a cheaper version stops being worth buying because the price carried the signal — option C.',92),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk, then answer the question: What does the woman show she understands at the end of the exchange?','Man: We usually treat price as a simple scoreboard of supply and demand, but there is a whole class of goods where the high price is itself part of the appeal. — Woman: You mean diamonds and handbags that cost more than their materials could ever explain? — Man: Exactly. Thorstein Veblen argued in 1899 that people sometimes buy things precisely because they are expensive, since the price broadcasts that the owner can afford to waste money. — Woman: So the demand curve bends backwards — raise the price and some buyers want it more. — Man: That is the odd part. Standard theory says a price rise thins the queue, but with these goods a price rise can leave the queue longer, because a cheaper version quietly stops being worth buying.','TTS','["That the goods are always luxuries","That Veblen disliked high prices","That supply drives every market","That a price rise can increase desire for these goods"]','D',1,'The woman paraphrases the twist herself — raise the price and some buyers want it more — which is option D.',93),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk, then answer the question: What misconception does the professor correct?','Professor: Last time we defined methylation as a chemical tag on the DNA strand. Today I want to stress what it is not. It is not a mutation; the letters of the genome stay exactly as they were. — Student: So if the code is unchanged, nothing really changes? — Professor: Think of a library where every book stays on the shelf, but some shelves are locked. The tag tells the cell''s reading machinery which stretches to skip, and that selection is what makes a liver cell and a neuron differ despite identical genomes. — Student: And the pattern survives division? — Professor: Largely, yes — a skin cell keeps making skin cells. But malnutrition and chronic stress can rewrite some tags, and a handful seem to pass to the next generation.','TTS','["That methylation changes the genetic code","That methylation occurs only in the brain","That cell division destroys all chemical tags","That mutations are inherited more often than tags"]','A',1,'The professor begins by insisting methylation is not a mutation and the code is unchanged, correcting the view in option A.',94),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk, then answer the question: Why does the professor use the library analogy?','Professor: Last time we defined methylation as a chemical tag on the DNA strand. Today I want to stress what it is not. It is not a mutation; the letters of the genome stay exactly as they were. — Student: So if the code is unchanged, nothing really changes? — Professor: Think of a library where every book stays on the shelf, but some shelves are locked. The tag tells the cell''s reading machinery which stretches to skip, and that selection is what makes a liver cell and a neuron differ despite identical genomes. — Student: And the pattern survives division? — Professor: Largely, yes — a skin cell keeps making skin cells. But malnutrition and chronic stress can rewrite some tags, and a handful seem to pass to the next generation.','TTS','["To show how identical content can be read selectively","To argue that genes are stored like books","To explain why libraries matter to medicine","To compare old DNA with new DNA"]','A',1,'Books on unchanged shelves with some locked illustrate reading the same content in different subsets — option A.',95),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk, then answer the question: What does the professor say about methylation patterns over time?','Professor: Last time we defined methylation as a chemical tag on the DNA strand. Today I want to stress what it is not. It is not a mutation; the letters of the genome stay exactly as they were. — Student: So if the code is unchanged, nothing really changes? — Professor: Think of a library where every book stays on the shelf, but some shelves are locked. The tag tells the cell''s reading machinery which stretches to skip, and that selection is what makes a liver cell and a neuron differ despite identical genomes. — Student: And the pattern survives division? — Professor: Largely, yes — a skin cell keeps making skin cells. But malnutrition and chronic stress can rewrite some tags, and a handful seem to pass to the next generation.','TTS','["They are erased at every division","They can never be altered once written","They pass only to nerve cells","They mostly persist through division yet can partly be rewritten by experience"]','D',1,'The professor says patterns largely survive division but stress and nutrition can rewrite some — option D.',96),
(@set08_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk, then answer the question: According to the professor, why do a liver cell and a neuron behave differently?','Professor: Last time we defined methylation as a chemical tag on the DNA strand. Today I want to stress what it is not. It is not a mutation; the letters of the genome stay exactly as they were. — Student: So if the code is unchanged, nothing really changes? — Professor: Think of a library where every book stays on the shelf, but some shelves are locked. The tag tells the cell''s reading machinery which stretches to skip, and that selection is what makes a liver cell and a neuron differ despite identical genomes. — Student: And the pattern survives division? — Professor: Largely, yes — a skin cell keeps making skin cells. But malnutrition and chronic stress can rewrite some tags, and a handful seem to pass to the next generation.','TTS','["Their DNA letters differ slightly between cell types","Neurons divide faster and keep fewer tags","The same genome is read in different subsets because tags mark regions to skip","Liver cells lose their methylation tags at division"]','C',1,'The professor attributes the difference to selection — tags telling the machinery which stretches to skip in each cell type — option C.',97);
INSERT INTO tp_questions (exam_id, section, task_type, prompt, body_text, audio_mode, choices_json, answer_key, auto_score, explanation, order_no) VALUES
(@set08_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["can","use","to","scientists","consumer","study","diamond","prices","behavior"]','scientists can use diamond prices to study consumer behavior',1,'English declarative order: subject (scientists) + verb phrase (can use diamond prices) + purpose clause (to study consumer behavior).',98),
(@set08_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["before","be","the","office","the","report","should","closes","submitted","grant"]','the report should be submitted before the grant office closes',1,'Passive main clause (the report should be submitted) followed by the time clause (before the grant office closes).',99),
(@set08_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["visitors","use","room","only","may","during","reading","pencils","archive","hours"]','reading room visitors may use only pencils during archive hours',1,'Subject noun phrase (reading room visitors) + modal (may use) + object with only (only pencils) + time phrase (during archive hours).',100),
(@set08_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["built","its","london","great","after","the","sewers","smelled","river","unbearable"]','london built its great sewers after the river smelled unbearable',1,'Main clause (london built its great sewers) + after-clause with linking verb (after the river smelled unbearable).',101),
(@set08_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["rises","the","of","slowly","land","weight","gone","ice","after","ancient","is"]','land rises slowly after the weight of ancient ice is gone',1,'Main clause (land rises slowly) + time clause (after the weight of ancient ice is gone).',102),
(@set08_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["journals","hide","names","so","that","reviewers","judge","only","the","written","work"]','journals hide names so that reviewers judge only the written work',1,'Main clause (journals hide names) + purpose clause introduced by so that (so that reviewers judge only the written work).',103),
(@set08_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["every","will","the","before","review","committee","table","budget","deadline","the"]','the committee will review every budget table before the deadline',1,'Subject (the committee) + verb phrase (will review) + object (every budget table) + time phrase (before the deadline).',104),
(@set08_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["marks","pass","cells","their","forms","to","methylation","new","each","that","cell"]','cells pass their methylation marks to each new cell that forms',1,'Subject (cells) + verb with indirect object (pass their methylation marks to each new cell) + relative clause (that forms).',105),
(@set08_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_EMAIL','Write an email to the grants office (about 50–80 words) explaining that one attachment in your application was the wrong version and asking how to replace it before the deadline. Include a greeting, your applicant number, and your name.',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review. TestPrep does not auto-score writing; aim for a clear request, polite tone and complete sentences.',106),
(@set08_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_EMAIL','Write an email to the archive reading room (about 50–80 words) asking to move your Tuesday appointment to Friday because of a lab re-certification session. Greet the clerk, mention the box number, and sign your name.',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review. A good answer states the conflict, names both dates, and closes courteously.',107),
(@set08_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_DISCUSSION','Your university plans to move the two-hour lab safety re-certification online instead of in person. Do you think this is a good idea? Why or why not? (about 100 words) — Danny: Online saves everyone an afternoon, and most of the content is slides we have already watched. — Maya: Slides cannot teach you what to do when a fume hood fails; the hands-on part is the whole point.',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review. State your position, give a reason, and respond to at least one of the two viewpoints.',108),
(@set08_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_DISCUSSION','Some journals now ask referees to sign their reviews instead of staying anonymous. Would signed review improve or harm academic publishing? (about 100 words) — Sofia: A signed referee would write more carefully, because their own reputation stands behind the words. — Raj: Famous labs would still receive gentler treatment, and a signature could scare junior critics away from honest disagreement.',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review. An effective answer takes a side, supports it with a developed reason, and engages with one of the two viewpoints quoted in the prompt.',109);
INSERT INTO tp_questions (exam_id, section, task_type, prompt, body_text, audio_mode, choices_json, answer_key, auto_score, explanation, order_no) VALUES
(@set08_exam_id,'SPEAKING','SPEAK_REPEAT','Listen to the sentence and repeat it aloud as clearly as possible. Record your answer.','The graduate school requires the defence form two weeks before the scheduled date.','TTS',NULL,NULL,0,'Recorded for manual review. Repeat with natural stress and complete words.',110),
(@set08_exam_id,'SPEAKING','SPEAK_REPEAT','Listen to the sentence and repeat it aloud as clearly as possible. Record your answer.','Please collect your badge from the east hall desk after the lunch break.','TTS',NULL,NULL,0,'Recorded for manual review. Keep the phrase groups smooth: east hall desk / after the lunch break.',111),
(@set08_exam_id,'SPEAKING','SPEAK_REPEAT','Listen to the sentence and repeat it aloud as clearly as possible. Record your answer.','All applications without a completed budget table are returned without being reviewed.','TTS',NULL,NULL,0,'Recorded for manual review. Stress the words completed and returned.',112),
(@set08_exam_id,'SPEAKING','SPEAK_REPEAT','Listen to the sentence and repeat it aloud as clearly as possible. Record your answer.','The reading room allows only pencils because ink can damage the manuscripts.','TTS',NULL,NULL,0,'Recorded for manual review. Pause lightly before because.',113),
(@set08_exam_id,'SPEAKING','SPEAK_REPEAT','Listen to the sentence and repeat it aloud as clearly as possible. Record your answer.','Members must repeat the full safety induction after their card has expired.','TTS',NULL,NULL,0,'Recorded for manual review. Stress full and expired.',114),
(@set08_exam_id,'SPEAKING','SPEAK_REPEAT','Listen to the sentence and repeat it aloud as clearly as possible. Record your answer.','The land around the gulf is still rising a few millimetres each year.','TTS',NULL,NULL,0,'Recorded for manual review. Keep millimetres clear without slowing the rest.',115),
(@set08_exam_id,'SPEAKING','SPEAK_REPEAT','Listen to the sentence and repeat it aloud as clearly as possible. Record your answer.','Blind reviewers judge the argument rather than the reputation of its author.','TTS',NULL,NULL,0,'Recorded for manual review. Stress argument and reputation.',116),
(@set08_exam_id,'SPEAKING','SPEAK_REPEAT','Listen to the sentence and repeat it aloud as clearly as possible. Record your answer.','Decades of advertising helped fix the diamond as the token of engagement.','TTS',NULL,NULL,0,'Recorded for manual review. Run decades of smoothly.',117),
(@set08_exam_id,'SPEAKING','SPEAK_INTERVIEW','Answer the interview question aloud (about 30–45 seconds). Record your answer. Some universities require lab safety re-certification every year; others renew cards for three years at a time. Which schedule do you think is better, and why? Give at least one reason.',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review. State your choice early, support it with a reason, and add one example.',118),
(@set08_exam_id,'SPEAKING','SPEAK_INTERVIEW','Answer the interview question aloud (about 30–45 seconds). Record your answer. A student newspaper claims that blind peer review should be replaced entirely by signed review. Do you agree or disagree, and what would be the main effect on young researchers?',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review. A strong answer takes a position, gives reasons, and considers the effect on junior scientists.',119),
(@set08_exam_id,'SPEAKING','SPEAK_INTERVIEW','Answer the interview question aloud (about 30–45 seconds). Record your answer. Cities sometimes spend heavily to move old sewer outfalls further downstream. Was that a wise use of public money? Explain with a reason and an example.',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review. Decide, justify with a reason, and finish with a concrete example.',120);
